CORE
main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ non-template constructor: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ constructor template: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ vtable pointer and NSDMI: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] line \d+ pack-expanded bases: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.5\] line \d+ value-initialized base: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.6\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [class.base.init]/9, /13: a constructor default-initializes the
bases and members its mem-initializer-list does not name and installs the
virtual pointers.  typecheck_compound_body completes the list of
non-template constructors (full_member_initialization); a constructor
template's specializations never got it, so a base's default constructor,
the members' default member initializers and the vtable pointer were
missing (bin53).  complete_constructor_template_initializers now
completes the list after prepare_deferred_method_body has expanded the
mem-initializer packs.  That needed two more fixes:
full_member_initialization matched `element<0, int>(a)' and `element<1,
long>(a)' to the same base by the name `element' ([class.base.init]/2:
a template-id denotes its specialization), and an empty mem-initializer
`B()' of a POD base did not value-initialize it ([dcl.init.general]/9).
Found while reducing libc++'s __tuple_impl.  g++ 13 and clang++ 18 accept
this program, and it fails only its WRONG assertion.
